Review 5320097003 (maxime-tt), item 2, verbatim: "The latency sentence
understates the path-release flow by roughly 2x. ADR-0004:312 says 'worst
case one extra 2500 ms per publish action' ... A single
publishBidderPathRelease call performs two sequential publish-path verdict
reads ... and both are reached on the same click whenever the verdict is
genuinely absent ... Worst case there is ≈ 2 × 2.4 s. In
publishAuctionSettlement the two reads ... sit in mutually exclusive
branches, so there it really is one window."

Verified at 72402cdd: `publishBidderPathRelease` reads publish-path
verdicts twice on one click — src/publish/auctions.tsx:1303 (the
`Promise.all` above the settlement check) and :1366 (the
`won_pending_settlement` quorum gate). Both are reached when the verdict is
genuinely absent, so the worst case is two windows, ≈ 2 × 2500 ms (each
read stops at the last step inside its own window, ≈ 2.4 s).
`publishAuctionSettlement` reads at :1653 (the `reserve_not_met` shortcut,
which returns) and :1737 (the winning-bid path) — mutually exclusive
branches, so one window there. The arithmetic stays consistent with the
amendment's own budget (2500 ms window / 300 ms step, ≤ 9 reads).
